WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.920
On the History Channel series Pawn Stars,
the experts at the World Famous Gold &amp; Silver

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.060
Pawn Shop in Las Vegas meet regular folks
with unusual objects to sell—sometimes worthless,

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.910
but often surprisingly valuable.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.590
Here are some of the biggest payouts in Pawn
Stars history…

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:17.380
Dutch East India Trading Company Bell

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.250
The woman who brought this 17th century ship's
bell into the shop was actually considering

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.170
putting it out in a yard sale.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:25.880
Richard "Old Man" Harrison thought the bell
had to be a reproduction.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:30.449
But the expert they brought in confirmed that
it was indeed a shipwreck bell from 1602,

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:32.189
and was worth a small fortune.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:35.430
"$15,000 give or take"

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.790
1715 Spanish Fleet Gold Coin

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:42.420
In 1715, a Spanish fleet carrying millions
of pesos worth of gold sank to the bottom

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.070
of the ocean off the coast of Florida.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:48.120
Lucky customer Jody inherited an exceptional
gold coin from this shipwreck.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.290
At first, Rick Harrison thought the coin was
in too good of a condition to be genuine,

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:55.120
but the expert confirmed that it was authentic
and was worth a cool $18,000.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:05.810
"From everything I can see on this one, I'd
say it's...absolutely genuine."

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:07.579
Joe Greene's Olympic Medals

00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:12.170
Olympian Jumpin' Joe Greene came across hard
times in the late '90s, so he sold the Harrisons

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:16.139
his Olympic bronze medals from the 1992 and
1996 games.

00:01:16.139 --> 00:01:20.299
Rick reportedly assured him that despite their
$30,000 value he would never sell the medals.

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:22.759
But in this world, everything has a price.

00:01:22.759 --> 00:01:27.899
"I'm asking $20,000 for the Atlanta one, $10,000
for the Barcelona."

00:01:27.899 --> 00:01:29.859
Order Of The White Eagle Medallion

00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:34.579
A very lucky yard sale fanatic picked up this
medal for only 75 cents and sold it to Rick

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:36.509
for a cool $6,000.

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:40.030
It turned out the medal dated back to the
era of Tsarist occupation of Poland, and Rick

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.810
eventually resold the medal for thirty-grand.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.479
Book Of Mormon

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:48.509
Adam got more than he bargained for when expert
Rebecca Romney appraised his 5th edition copy

00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:50.189
of the Book of Mormon from 1842.

00:01:50.189 --> 00:01:56.400
"I would appraise this book actually at about
$40,000."

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.960
Rick and Adam finally settled on a purchase
price of $24,000, and both walked away happy

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.929
with the transaction.

00:02:02.929 --> 00:02:05.479
1554 Spanish Shipwreck Gold Bar

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:09.330
When one man was cleaning out the belongings
of his grandfather, he found a gold bar hidden

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:10.429
away in a box.

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:15.239
Its melt-down value alone was $24,000, but
when an expert confirmed it was from a 1554

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:17.319
Spanish shipwreck, the price doubled instantly.

00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:20.170
"So you're telling me that's worth $48,000?"

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:21.269
"In that neighborhood."

00:02:21.269 --> 00:02:22.650
"Yahaha!"

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.590
2001 Patriots Super Bowl Ring

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:29.860
Patriots defensive back Brock Williams hit
a hard patch and he decided to pawn his 2001

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.290
Patriots Super Bowl ring for a modest $2,600.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:36.450
He never came back for it, and the ring became
the property of the shop.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.689
While similar rings have sold for around $30,000
on auction websites, Rick isn't ready to let

00:02:40.689 --> 00:02:44.329
go of his favorite item any time soon, which
is why he's set the sale price on this beauty

00:02:44.329 --> 00:02:47.010
to a whopping $100,000.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.260
John F. Kennedy's Cigar Box

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.200
The biggest payout ever on Pawn Stars is thanks
to a piece of presidential history.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.020
A seller walked into the shop with a box of
cigars that once sat on John F. Kennedy's

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.650
desk in the Oval Office.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:03.299
After some haggling, Rick managed to purchase
JFK's cigar box for $60,000.

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:07.620
For comparison, a similar JFK humidor was
purchased by the publisher of Cigar Aficionado

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:12.250
in 1996 for $575,000.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:13.250
Thanks for watching!

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.689
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:15.689 --> 00:03:18.610
And leave us a comment to let us know what
you think of these Pawn Stars deals…

